 \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-216710/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-216710"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-211004"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/0a23346ac28172521a3230a949607422efe77e2803973ac42e4255f321c3a1a0.png)\n\nFind the root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $5$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $5$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{5}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/5}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/53c8822760ceb51c1d1bd685947dbfefa7dfdc3490027db62cbcaf5d9aa24335.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=2.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{2}={e}^{2(2)\\pi \\text{i}/5}={e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-216706/selection/a193e57d5