-216706" :question/id #uuid "23ae02b3-e88e-5a0a-9b61-10fa9eac66c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-216706" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/89d0606f11701cb59685e5ea1540b8ed98ce8cfe371951a675f01a1c6cc5a748.png)\n\nFind the $7$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/344d760f2cd08e5e1e1eccc2664e7182d01744ee9b0b15f985c6d7b4e0e5515d.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=3.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{3}={e}^{2(3)\\pi \\text{i}/7}={e}^{6\\pi \\text{i}/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-216706/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b8874325-af33-506b-b24d-3a3e387bd88c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-216706"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-211001"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/0c592ccfd6ea92e0f0181d5dfe4804cc0dba800f468d9fb4e9d96cef2be72be4.png)\n\nFind the $6$th root of unity highlighted in the diagram above." :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $r{e}^{\\text{i}\\theta }.$ Notice that $|1|=1$ and $\\operatorname{arg}⁡(1)=0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1={e}^{\\text{i}(0+2k\\pi )}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/ff5d1682ca8d142ad4cf73b9a5c996164653d940157efd695d1da13d34900404.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=4.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{4}={e}^{(4)\\pi \\text{i}/3}={e}^{4\\pi \\text{i}/3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-14557"] :question/worked-solution "Let $z$ be a $6$th root of unity. This means that \n\n$$\nz^{6} = 1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}.$ Notice that $|1| = 1$ and $\\arg(1) = 0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{6}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/6}={e}^{k\\pi \\text{i}/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe principal root corresponds to $k=1,$ and is therefore equal to $e^{\\pi \\textrm{i}/3}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-14558"] :question/worked-solution "Geometrically, the $4$th roots of unity have the following properties:\n\n- all of them lie on the unit circle centered in the origin,\n\n- one of the roots is $1,$ corresponding to the point $(1,0)$ on the complex plane,\n\n- the roots form a square (regular $4$-sided polygon).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/9111f21143412d791b0195722da4f6d461444f386efd9baf609c70a4fe709fd8.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-14559"] :question/problem "Find the root of unity highlighted in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/7ae59d6d770f5cb0ef10a42c6bc9e5851fa48b1d122417ce78155a8dcf17be6a.png)" :question/worked-solution "The $n$th roots of unity lie at the vertices of a regular $n$-gon inscribed inside the unit circle in the complex plane. One of the vertices of this regular $n$-gon must lie at the point $(1,0).$\n\nSince there are $7$ points in our diagram, we need to find a $7$th root of unity.\n\nFirst, we express the complex number $1$ in the form $re^{\\textrm{i}\\theta}.$ Notice that $|1| = 1$ and $\\arg(1) = 0.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n1 = e^{\\textrm{i}(0 + 2k\\pi)} = e^{2k\\pi \\textrm{i}},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is an integer. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}}\\,⟹\\,z=({e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}})^{1/7}={e}^{2k\\pi \\text{i}/7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the index $k$ of our root of unity, we enumerate the roots in the diagram counter-clockwise, starting with the root at $(1,0)$ corresponding to $k=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980994/assets/730b0c3c63799b9101ce89d38e3d96c10bc0e217b967506393ea67757ecf48d7.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the required root corresponds to $k=5.$ Substituting this into the expression for roots, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}_{5}={e}^{2(5)\\pi \\text{i}/7}={e}^{10\\pi \\text{i}/7}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
